< Introduction >
 
This only works with MySQL at the moment.
If you want to make it work with other databases, please feel free to do so.

Firstly, I did not right this script from scratch, I took MyNews as a base and HEAVILY modified, rewrote,
documented and cleaned up his code.  It works in a completely different fashion and doesn't look at all
like MyNews anymore.  But Mike McMurray still deserves credit.  He also gives credit in his README:

"First thing I would like to do is to give credit where it is due. I did
not write this script from scratch, however I did make major modifications
to it, and wrote new subroutines to execute different things within this
script. So right now, I would like to thank Stig-Ørjan Smelror of
winther.net (stig@winter.net) who if it weren't for his origional concept
I probably never would have persued this!"


< Requirements >

	* Greater than or equal to PHP 4.0.0
	* Greater than or equal to MySQL 3.22 (lower version may work)
	* A Web Server (I suggest Apache)
	* A somewhat functional brain (not required but recommended)


< Features >

	* Posting, editing, deleting new and old news items
	* Administration via the WWW
	* Unique logins and access levels
	* Basic news archiving


< Installation >

1.  Create a new mysql database with a name of your choice, usually 'nune' or 'news'.  If you don't have permission to create a new database, have an administrator do it.

Example:

prompt> mysql -u root -p
(enter password)
mysql> create database nune;
mysql> exit;

2.  Create a special user with access to the database, usually called 'nunemgr'.

Example:

prompt> mysql -u root -p
(enter password)
mysql> GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON nune.* TO nunemgr@localhost IDENTIFIED BY 'some_pass';
mysql> exit;

Note: If you have a MySQL user and that user is the one with privileges to your nune database, then you can skip this step.

3.  Input the nune.sql file into your nune databases.

Example:

prompt> mysql -u nunemgr -p nune < nune.sql
(enter what you put as some_pass above)


< Configuration >

Whew!  Now you're done the hard part.  

1.  Go to the news/admin/config directory and edit nune.conf.  The file is completely documented so it should be no problem. 

2.  Move the news/ directory to your web area...

Example:

prompt> mv news /usr/local/share/httpd

3.  See if you did everything right.  Go to your favourite browser and look at the page.

Example:

prompt> w3m http://www.mylamepage.com/news/index.php

4.  It's probably a good idea to include the index.php inside a page.  You should know how to do this on your own, but here's how you would do it with PHP.

Example:

<? include('news/index.php'); ?>  

5.  Let me know about any problems.  Enjoy!

NOTE: For some reason, you can't edit the included post (the introduction).  No big deal.  Just to let you homees know.


< Troubleshooting >

Q1.  I get the error: "Parse error: parse error in lib/nune-lib.php on line 115" when I login to NUNE.

A1.  Well, the requirements for NUNE do specify PHP4, and that particular line uses a PHP4-only function,
     so that's probably the problem.  If you absolutely can not upgrade to PHP4, then you can do the following:

     Replace:

     Line 115: foreach ($archive as $key => $value) {

     with

     Line 115: while (list($key, $value) = each ($archive)) {

     The two lines are functionally identical, with the exception that one is PHP4 specific.


